          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Abraham:</strong> There has been a significant growth in cyber criminality over the last few years especially with ransomware. Breaches of personal data leaves victims vulnerable to fraud and puts lives at risk. Business is the main target although phishing attacks on individuals are increasing, however more people are becoming aware of the threat. On a global scale governments and nations are also at high risk of falling victim to cyber crime in one way or another and this affects everyone.</p>
<p><strong>Nicole:</strong> Cybercrime is a violation of an individual's privacy and their data security. They can be at risk of hacking, malware, identity theft, financial fraud, medical fraud, as well as interpersonal cybercrime such as cyber bullying. Data is considered a commodity and is a primary target of cybercriminals. It can be accessed illicitly if not adequately protected by internet security systems or given away to scammers unwittingly by individuals clicking onto bogus links and it can be used to commit further cybercrimes.</p>
<p><strong>Abraham:</strong> Businesses are at risk from Phishing, DDoS, and malware. Malware can include different subtypes such as ransomware, trojans, worms, crypto miners or spyware as a few examples. The attack type will often depend on the type of business and the technology they use. It will also be dependent on what information the hacker wants to extract or attack. Cyber attacks can be, as we have already discussed, by external or insider threats. Trojan malware enables hackers to create a backdoor into the business’ systems and access business data and login credentials. Social engineering covers a broad range of malicious cyber crimes manipulating users into making security mistakes or giving away sensitive information or granting access to resources. Hackers can inject malicious code into a website to gain access to information and login details through keyloggers and hacking a database of passwords.</p>
<p><strong>Nicole:</strong> There has been a significant growth in high-profile ransomware campaigns. Ransomware involves paying a hefty fee for releasing their data, however this does not always happen even when the fee has been paid. There’s been a recent increase in ransomware because companies have started to use cyber insurance. If a company is affected by ransomware then the insurance company will pay the ransom, so cybercriminals know that it is very likely they will get the money. There is, however, no guarantee that the criminals will unencrypt the data even after they are paid.</p>
<p><strong>Abraham:</strong> This can have a knock on effect, an example of this is when the AXA insurance firm was ‘ransomwared’ in response to them publicly announcing they will no longer offer ‘pay out’ clauses for cyber insurance</p>
<p>It's common now to hear on the news about cyber attacks from foreign governments like North Korea, Russia and China, but threats don't only come from recognised countries.</p>
<p>Cyber attacks at international and national state level can involve major organisations, national infrastructure, political parties, military, and governments.</p>
<p><strong>Nicole:</strong> The vulnerabilities for nations are often the same and under the same threats as businesses, but these can take on much greater significance as they can create consequences for everyone. The threats from nation states and criminal groups are starting to become blurred as some nation states use the 'facilities' provided by criminal groups for their own 'state run' attacks. Many Russian-speaking cyber groups are threatening UK interests, but home-grown cyber criminals are also becoming more sophisticated and offering their services to others.</p>
<p><strong>Abraham:</strong> The increasing ability for purchasing 'cyber tools' has enabled emerging threats to become established quickly. Cyber security attacks have become the new weapon of choice for many nation states in the effort to de-stabilise their opponents. Attacks and threats may be of a political nature trying to skew the outcomes of elections, financial gain, digital surveillance to taking over of military capabilities and rendering nations helpless in the face of the threat.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
